WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:06.800
Leslie David Baker, who portrayed salesman Stanley&nbsp;
Hudson, is only one of five actors to appear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:11.920
in every single episode of "The Office." Though&nbsp;
Stanley might not be the first character that fans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:16.960
might associate with the series, he joins names&nbsp;
like Dwight Schrute, played by Rainn Wilson, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:23.120
Jim Halpert, portrayed by John Krasinski, on that&nbsp;
exclusive list of true Dunder Mifflin mainstays.

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:28.240
While "The Office" came to a close in 2013,&nbsp;
Baker's career remains defined in large part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:33.040
by the show. In fact, Baker revealed to&nbsp;
Tomorrow Comes Movies at the 2018 Los&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:38.160
Angeles Comic-Con that he would be open to an&nbsp;
"Office" reunion, and he even began attempting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:43.520
to finance a spin-off about Stanley through&nbsp;
Kickstarter in 2020. In fact, the Kickstarter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:49.040
campaign ultimately raised enough money to shoot&nbsp;
a pilot for the series, titled "Uncle Stan." The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:53.120
show aimed to focus on the relationship&nbsp;
between Stanley and his nephew, Lucky,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:58.560
who comes to his uncle for guidance when he starts&nbsp;
up his own hybrid florist-motorcycle business.

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:04.480
Sadly, since 2020, there's been no word on&nbsp;
"Uncle Stan." However, like most of his co-stars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:09.520
Baker continues to enjoy the enduring passion of&nbsp;
fans of "The Office," even if that means indulging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:13.840
in some of their less than convincing&nbsp;
theories that are widely shared online.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:19.520
Speaking of one such, pretty unbelievable theory:&nbsp;
what if Stanley isn't merely a reliable supporting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:24.560
player in "The Office," but actually the show's&nbsp;
low-key most important character? According to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:29.520
an highly unlikely, yet oddly compelling&nbsp;
fan theory shared on Reddit, approximately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:34.960
half of "The Office" episodes might take place&nbsp;
not in reality, but within Stanley's psyche.

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:39.440
According to a Reddit user who appropriately&nbsp;
shared their idea to the "FanTheories" subreddit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:43.840
a Season 5 episode of "The Office" titled&nbsp;
"Stress Relief" might be the most important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:48.320
one in the show's run. In the episode's cold&nbsp;
open, Dwight purposefully lights a fire in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:52.880
trash can in order to set off the office's&nbsp;
smoke alarms and test his co-workers' fire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:56.880
preparedness. In the ensuing chaos,&nbsp;
Stanley suffers a heart attack.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:01.360
He ultimately appears to survive without&nbsp;
issue and is back at work after a short break.

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:05.520
"You will not die! Stanley,&nbsp;
Stanley, you will not die!"

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:10.400
The Redditor, however, theorizes that Stanley's&nbsp;
heart attack was fatal. Everything in "The Office"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:15.040
from the moments following the opening credits&nbsp;
of "Stress Relief" to its series finale represent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:20.880
Stanley in hell, and his supposed co-workers are&nbsp;
now demons imitating his onetime officemates.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:25.600
This theory hinges on two assumptions: that&nbsp;
the mishaps in Dunder Mifflin are particularly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:31.120
torturous for Stanley, and that the behaviors&nbsp;
of various characters shift significantly in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:35.680
and after "Stress Relief." In the wildly&nbsp;
farfetched theory, the Redditor argues,

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:40.320
"Stanley was the person who enjoyed being at the&nbsp;
office the least [...] most often refusing to take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:44.960
part in whatever shenanigans were being cooked&nbsp;
up. Hell for him is the office [...] but worse."

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:47.840
"I'm going to die."

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:53.520
One could argue, if you buy the initial theory,&nbsp;
that vague hints are placed throughout the show.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:58.080
The most notable takes place in the Season 6&nbsp;
episode "Secret Santa." There's an exchange&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:03.360
between Stanley and Michael Scott, where Stanley&nbsp;
opens a gift, a scented candle, and Michael taunts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:08.160
Stanley with the argument that he'll be facing&nbsp;
punishment for cheating on his wife. Michael says,

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:10.880
"A lot of fire where you're&nbsp;
going. Better get used to it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:15.520
You're going to H-E-L-L double hockey&nbsp;
sticks. Going to hell, Stanley."

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:19.760
To be fair, Stanley does appear to&nbsp;
be perpetually grumpy. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:23.680
as with so many fan theories, this&nbsp;
one begins to collapse under scrutiny,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:28.320
since plenty of moments that ensue, both&nbsp;
good and bad, don't concern him whatsoever.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:33.280
Some other Reddit users, as usual, raised&nbsp;
concerns with the theory. For instance, one wrote,

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:37.200
"The last time I checked hell doesn't&nbsp;
reward people with free meatballs."

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:41.520
Alternatively, another user took&nbsp;
things to the next level and wrote,

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:45.040
"Creed is clearly satan if&nbsp;
your theory holds any validity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:50.000
Killed a guy that crossed him and took his&nbsp;
name and identity. No morals to speak of."

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:52.640
"I feel like I'm working at my own casket."

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:57.840
The truth is that fan theories are purely&nbsp;
theories. They usually don't make sense, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:03.360
they provide a fun way to re-watch old episodes.&nbsp;
And as it is with many fan theories, the creative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:08.320
minds behind "The Office" almost certainly did&nbsp;
not plan most of the show's run to make it look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:13.120
like it was Stanley's personal hell, particularly&nbsp;
since there is no such revelation contained in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:17.120
final episode. Nonetheless, a watch-through&nbsp;
of episodes after Stanley's heart attack,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:22.560
with this theory in mind, can shed entirely new&nbsp;
light onto what is, for many, an old favorite.

